# Dragon: Complete LLM Language Reference

Dragon is a statically typed, compiled language that reads like Python and compiles
through LLVM to a self-contained native binary. You write `.dr` files with braces
(or typed `.py` files with indentation), annotate every binding, and get C-class
machine code: no interpreter, no VM, no JIT warm-up, no boxing of known types.

This file is the one-stop language reference for LLMs and tools. It covers the
language surface, semantics, build/CLI, ownership, concurrency, templates, FFI,
and testing. It does NOT catalog the standard library (look modules up separately
in `stdlib/*.dr` or the docs site); the stdlib types that are part of how the
language is used (Task, Lock, TestCase, HTML, SQL) appear here only as far as the
language story needs them.

This file describes the language as designed. Its claims were checked by
compiling and running probe programs against the compiler (2026-08-27), so the
examples are real code, not aspiration.

## 1. Design rules (why the language is shaped this way)

1. **Speed is king.** The hot path is sacred; if it is interpreted, it is not
   sacred. Values whose type is known flow AT that type, in registers, never boxed.
2. **No workarounds; fix at the root.** A silent fallback is a silent lie. You
   should know it is broken when you compile.
3. **Types must be honest.** `Any` is a last resort, never a fallback. Ambiguity
   is a compile error to annotate, not a box the compiler picks for you.

Dragon borrows Python's surface only where it compiles cleanly to fast static
code. It rejects the dynamic crutches outright: `eval`/`exec`, `getattr`/`setattr`/
`hasattr`, `__dict__`, monkey-patching, metaclasses, runtime module loading,
untyped `*args`/`**kwargs`. These are refused by design, not gaps to be filled.

## 2. Toolchain: the `dragon` CLI

`dragon` (alias `dr`) is the single entry point.

```
dragon run   <file|dir> [-- args]   # compile to a temp binary, run it, clean up
dragon build <file|dir> -o app      # compile to a native executable
dragon check <file>                 # lex + parse + type check only (no codegen)
dragon repl                         # interactive JIT session (no linker); -f for the .py surface
dragon migrate <file.py>            # emit a typed .dr draft next to the input
dragon ffi sync <file> [--check]    # (re)generate foreign stubs for process externs
```

Egg / package verbs (D022): `dragon init` scaffolds a `dragon.drs` manifest in the
current directory (works today); `grab <name>`, `sync`, `drop <name>` are wired as
verbs but marked in progress. Until the egg manager is complete, you vendor
dependencies: copy the `.dr` files into your tree or point `-I` at them.

A `<dir>` argument to run/build resolves the `entry` field of that directory's
`dragon.drs`.

Flags:

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `-o <file>` | output path (default: source name with `.dr` stripped) |
| `-O0`..`-O3` | optimization level (default `-O0`) |
| `--release` | optimize aggressively (use for anything you measure or ship) |
| `-g` | debug info |
| `-f` | force Python-surface parsing (for `.py` content) |
| `-I <dir>` | add a module search path (repeatable) |
| `--site-packages` | search Python site-packages for typed `.py` modules (last resort) |
| `--gc=rc` | refcounting + cycle collector (the default) |
| `--gc=none` | no refcount emission; OS reclaims at exit. Short-lived programs only, never services |
| `-l<name>` / `-L<dir>` | link `lib<name>` / add a library search dir (FFI) |
| `--cc-source <file>` | compile and link a C/C++ shim (repeatable; a `.cpp` switches the link driver to `c++`) |
| `--dump-ast` / `--dump-tokens` | print the parser/lexer output and exit |
| `--backend <llvm>` | accepted for compatibility and ignored (LLVM is the only backend) |
| `-v, --verbose` | verbose output |

Environment: `DRAGON_STDLIB_PATH` overrides the bundled stdlib root (for working
on the stdlib itself).

The REPL accepts multi-line cells (Alt+Enter or Esc,Enter runs a cell), echoes
the value of a bare expression, and has `:help`, `:reset`, `:quit`/`:q`. It is
itself written in Dragon (`tools/repl/*.dr`) and JIT-compiles cells.

`dragon migrate` output is a DRAFT: untyped parameters come out as `Any` and the
inferred return types can be wrong; you are expected to fix the annotations.

### Compilation model

Pipeline: `Lexer -> Parser -> [TypeHintEnforcer, .py only] -> Sema -> TypeChecker
-> CodeGen (LLVM IR) -> cc/c++ link`. The module resolver walks the whole import
graph and lowers everything into ONE LLVM module: whole-program optimization,
direct cross-module calls, no separate compilation. The binary statically links
the Dragon runtime and depends only on libc/libm.

Value representation: `int` is `i64`, `float` is `f64`, `bool` is `i1`; `str`,
`bytes`, containers and instances are pointers; only `Union[...]` and `Any` are a
16-byte `{tag, payload}` box. Generics monomorphize: each distinct type argument
stamps out a separate native specialization (`Box[int]` and `Box[str]` are two
concrete types in the binary).

## 3. Program model: the file IS the program

- The file you hand to `dragon run`/`build` is the program; its top-level
  statements execute top to bottom. There is no `main`, no entry function, and no
  `__name__` (writing `__name__` is a compile error). A function named `main` is
  ordinary and runs only if called.
- Every other file is an import. An imported module contributes its definitions
  (functions, classes, consts, module globals with their initializers). The
  initializers run once at program start, in dependency order, BEFORE the entry
  file's body. A bare side-effecting statement at a dependency's top level (a
  loose `print(...)`) is silently skipped: a module is a bag of definitions, not
  a script.
- Forward references work: top-level code may call a function defined later in
  the file; the whole module's names resolve before codegen.

## 4. Two surfaces, one language

| | `.dr` (canonical) | `.py` (adoption ramp) |
|---|---|---|
| Blocks | braces `{ }` | indentation |
| Constructor | nameless `def(params) { ... }` | `def __init__(self, ...) -> None:` |
| `self` in methods | implicit (do NOT declare it) | explicit parameter, as in Python |
| `super` | `super(args)` / `super.method()` | `super().__init__(args)` / `super().method()` |
| `static` module vars | `static x: T = ...` | plain module var (same lifetime) |
| Types | mandatory | mandatory (PEP 484, enforced by a dedicated pass) |

Semantics are identical: block scoping, `:`-declares/`=`-reassigns, fixed types.
The constructor spellings are mode-exclusive and enforced: `def __init__` inside
a `.dr` class is a compile error ("The constructor is a nameless def"), and
`def area(self)` in `.dr` is a compile error telling you to drop `self`.
`.py` mode is for porting an individually typed Python file; it is NOT a promise
that arbitrary Python runs (numpy/pandas/Flask/CPython extensions never will).

In `.dr` source, `true`, `false`, and `none` are accepted aliases for `True`,
`False`, `None` (both spellings work).

## 5. Bindings: declare with `:`, reassign with `=`

```dragon
total: int = 0      # declares total, type fixed as int forever in this scope
total = total + 5   # reassigns; bare = requires the name to already exist
```

- A bare `=` to an undeclared name is a compile error (not a silent new
  variable). A misspelled assignment target cannot silently create a fresh local.
- A variable's type is fixed at declaration; assigning an incompatible type is a
  compile error.
- Redeclaring the same name twice in one scope is an error; reassign instead.
- Statement-level walrus declaration works and infers the type:
  `xs := [1, 2, 3]` declares `xs: list[int]`; `n := 5` declares `n: int`.
  (Inside an expression, `:=` is the usual walrus: `while (w := f()) != "stop"`.)
- Tuple unpacking is an implicit typed declaration: `lo, hi = minmax(xs)`.

### `const` and `static`

```dragon
const port: int = 2018          # single assignment; reassignment is a compile error
static cache: dict[str, str] = {}   # module-level, program lifetime, shared everywhere
```

`const` is a binding modifier, not deep immutability: a `const lst: list[int]`
still allows `lst.append(1)`; only rebinding `lst` is forbidden. `static` is
`.dr`-only (a `.py` module-level var already has that lifetime). `static const`
combines on class members for class constants.

### Definite assignment (enforced)

The compiler performs flow-sensitive definite-assignment analysis:

```dragon
result: int
if cond { result = 1 } else { result = 2 }
print(result)                   # ok: assigned on every path

result2: int
if cond { result2 = 1 }
print(result2)                  # COMPILE ERROR: may be read before it is assigned
```

The same analysis covers constructors: every field without a default must be
assigned on every path through the constructor, or the class does not compile
("constructor of 'A' may return without assigning field 'x'").

## 6. Scoping

- Dragon scopes by BLOCK, not by function: every `{ }` body (and every indented
  suite in `.py` mode) is its own scope: `if`, `elif`, `else`, `while`, `for`,
  `with`, `try`, `except`, `case` bodies included. A name declared inside a block
  vanishes at the closing brace. Sibling blocks can reuse a name freely.
- Shadowing across scopes is legal (an inner block may redeclare an outer name,
  even at a different type; the outer binding is untouched after the block).
- The block rule stops at the FUNCTION boundary:
  - Writing a module global from inside a function requires `global name`
    (a bare `=`/`+=` without it is a compile error). Reading needs no keyword.
  - Rebinding an ENCLOSING FUNCTION's local from a nested function/lambda
    requires `nonlocal name`; without it the assignment is a compile error that
    names both fixes. Reading captures freely.
  - Blocks inside the same function need no keyword; they are not a boundary.
- Only a top-level declaration is a module global; a declaration nested in a
  top-level `if`/`for` is block-local.

## 7. The type system

### Core annotations

```dragon
count:   int              = 42        # 64-bit signed; WRAPS on overflow, no OverflowError
ratio:   float            = 3.14      # IEEE 754 double
ok:      bool             = True      # its own type, not an int alias
label:   str              = "Dragon"  # immutable Unicode, len() counts code points
data:    bytes            = b"\x00"   # immutable byte sequence; b[i] is an int 0-255
ints:    list[int]        = [1, 2, 3]
prices:  dict[str, float] = {"pen": 1.5}
pair:    tuple[int, str]  = (1, "x")
tags:    set[str]         = {"a", "b"}
maybe:   int | None       = none
either:  int | str        = 42
anything: Any             = 42
f:       Callable[[int], int]         # a function value; [] arg list, then return
```

Type conversions are explicit: `int(x)`, `float(x)`, `str(x)`, `bool(x)`.
`int(s)`/`float(s)` raise `ValueError` on a bad string. `/` on ints yields
`float`; storing it into an `int` is a compile error (use `//`).

Type aliases: `type UserId = int` is a zero-cost compile-time synonym.
`type Handler = Callable[[Request], Response]` is the idiom for long signatures.

### Containers are monomorphized (layout is part of the type)

`list[int]` is a flat `int64[]`; `list[float]` a `double[]`; `list[str]` an array
of refcounted pointers; `list[Any]` an array of 16-byte boxes. These are different
memory layouts, so a NAMED concrete list never converts to `list[Any]` (or back)
by annotation; the compiler refuses the assignment and the call-site. Cross the
boundary with a fresh literal (built in the right layout from birth) or an
element-wise copy. A bare `Any` parameter, by contrast, accepts ANY list and
dispatches per element at runtime; that is the right shape for "takes any list".

Empty/mixed literals cannot infer an element type: `ys: list[int] = []` (annotate)
or `zs: list[Any] = [1, "a"]` (opt into the box explicitly).

Dicts are monomorphic in their key type: `dict[str, V]` and `dict[int, V]` are
distinct; one dict cannot hold both `1` and `"1"` as keys. Integer keys hash as
native `i64`.

### `T | None` is free; `Union` is a box; narrowing recovers the type

- For pointer-shaped `T` (str, bytes, containers, instances), `T | None` compiles
  to one nullable pointer: zero cost. Test with `is none` / `is not none`.
- `Optional[T]` (from `from typing import Optional`) is the same type; the bare
  `T | None` form is canonical and needs no import.
- A union of two non-None types (`int | str`) is a 16-byte tagged box. You cannot
  use it as either type directly; you narrow first:

```dragon
def describe(x: int | str) -> str {
    if isinstance(x, int) {
        return f"number: {x + 1}"       # x is a native int here
    } else {
        return f"text: {x.upper()}"     # else-branch narrows to the complement
    }
}
```

- `match` narrows the same way, and DOES narrow the subject inside the case body:

```dragon
match v {                       # v: int | str
    case int() { return f"num {v + 1}" }
    case str() { return f"text {v.upper()}" }
}
```

- `isinstance` is the narrowing tool: it narrows union-typed and Any-typed
  values by runtime tag, and it walks class hierarchies (`isinstance(dog,
  Animal)` is True). Use it to narrow unions/Any and to test class ancestry,
  not to interrogate values whose type is already statically known (the
  compiler already knows; there is nothing to ask).

### `Any`: the bounded dynamic tier

`Any` holds anything (16-byte box), reassignable across types. To do anything
with it you must narrow via `isinstance` (or `match`). The headline use is
`dict[str, Any]` for genuinely heterogeneous data (JSON-shaped). Never widen a
knowable type to `Any`: a spurious `Any` boxes, allocates, and hides bugs; in
this codebase it is treated as a defect. There is no `type(x)`-returning-class,
no `getattr`, no attribute discovery; `type(x)` returns the type NAME as a `str`
(e.g. `"int"`).

### `TypedDict`: a dict with a fixed schema

```dragon
class User(TypedDict) {
    name: str
    age:  int
}
u: User = User(name="Ada", age=36)
print(u.name)      # dot access, typed str
print(u["age"])    # subscript also works
```

A TypedDict IS a dict underneath (spread, `.items()`, passing where a dict is
expected all work); use it when the key set is fixed, a plain `dict[K, V]` when
keys are dynamic. `list[User]` composes; `users[0].name` is typed.

### FFI boundary types

`intc` lowers to C's 32-bit `int`; use it ONLY in `extern "C"` signatures (it
widens back to `int` in Dragon code). `ptr` is a raw `void*` for opaque handles;
the GC never touches a bare `ptr`. See section 25.

## 8. Operators

| Category | Operators | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Arithmetic | `+ - * / // % **`, unary `-` `+` | `/` ALWAYS yields float; `//` floors toward -inf; `%` takes the divisor's sign; `**` right-assoc, `-2 ** 2` is `-4`; int wraps silently at 64 bits |
| Comparison | `== != < > <= >=` | chainable: `1 < x < 10` (middle evaluated once) |
| Boolean | `and` `or` `not` | short-circuit; `and`/`or` evaluate to the OPERAND they stop on, not a bool |
| Bitwise | `& \| ^ ~ << >>` | ints only; C precedence (`&` > `^` > `\|`) |
| Membership | `in` / `not in` | value membership; substring test on str (the only way, no `.contains()`) |
| Identity | `is` / `is not` | for `none` tests |
| Bind-in-expr | `:=` | walrus; parenthesize inside larger expressions |
| Ternary | `x if cond else y` | right-associative |
| Augmented | `+= -= *= /= //= %= **= &= \|= ^= <<= >>=` | statement level |

`+` also concatenates str/list/bytes; `*` repeats str/list (`[0] * 4`). Set
algebra works both as operators and methods: `a | b`, `a & b`, `a - b`,
`a.union(b)`, `a.intersection(b)`, `a.difference(b)`, `a.symmetric_difference(b)`;
`< <= > >=` on sets test (proper) subset/superset.

`and`/`or` operand-typing: both operands same type -> that type; int with float
promotes to float; unrelated types produce the union you must annotate
(`either: int | str = 0 or "x"`). `or` drops `None` from the left type, so
`label: str = lookup(k) or "default"` needs no narrowing. Falsy values: `0`,
`0.0`, `False`, `""`, empty list/dict/set, `None`.

`@` is ONLY the decorator sigil (no matmul). `not` binds looser than comparison
(`not a == b` is `not (a == b)`).

Precedence, loosest to tightest: ternary; `or`; `and`; `not`; comparisons
(chained); `|`; `^`; `&`; `<< >>`; `+ -`; `* / // %`; unary `- + ~`; `**`;
call/subscript/attribute.

## 9. Control flow

- `if` / `elif` / `else`. Conditions must be `bool`: truthiness is NOT automatic
  in statement conditions (`if 0 { }` is a type error; write `if n != 0`).
- `while cond { }`, `for x in iterable { }`, `break`, `continue`, and the Python
  `else` clause on loops (runs only when no `break` fired).
- `for` iterates lists, tuples, sets, dicts (keys, insertion order), strings
  (one-char strings), bytes (each byte as an `int`), generators, `range`, and
  any `__iter__`/`__next__` object. `for k, v in d.items()` unpacks;
  `enumerate(xs)` and `zip(a, b)` drive loops.
- `pass` is the explicit empty statement.

### `match` (statement)

```dragon
match value {
    case 0 { print("zero") }
    case "" { print("empty") }
    case int() { print("some other int") }        # type test; narrows the subject in the body
    case int() | bool() { print("numberish") }    # OR pattern
    case [a, b] { print(a + b) }                  # sequence (list or tuple form)
    case Point(x, y) { print(f"{x},{y}") }        # class positional destructuring (works)
    case n if n > 100 { print("big") }            # capture + guard
    case _ { print("anything else") }             # wildcard
}
```

Verified: literals, type tests (runtime tag for Union/Any subjects; non-null
check for `Class | None`; subclass matches its base), OR patterns, sequence
patterns, capture, wildcard, guards (`case x if cond`), and class positional
destructuring `case Point(x, y)` all work. NOT supported: dict/mapping patterns
(`case {"a": v}` is a parse error). `.dr` arms use braces with no colon.
First match wins; no fall-through.

## 10. Strings, f-strings, bytes

`str` is immutable Unicode; `len`, indexing, and slicing work in code points
(`len("café")` is 4). Single, double, and triple quotes; the usual escapes.
Indexing yields a one-character `str`; negative indices and `[a:b]`, `[a:b:c]`,
`[::-1]` slices as in Python.

Method set (Python-shaped): `upper lower title strip lstrip rstrip find rfind
count startswith endswith split rsplit splitlines join partition replace rjust
ljust center zfill isalpha isdigit isalnum isspace isupper islower encode
capitalize`. `find` returns `-1` for no match. `split()` with no argument splits
on whitespace runs. `s.encode("utf-8")` <-> `b.decode("utf-8")`; codecs are
deliberately just `utf-8` (aliases `utf8`, `u8`) and `ascii` (`us-ascii`), other
names raise `LookupError`; pass `"replace"` as error handler to avoid raising
(`UnicodeDecodeError`/`UnicodeEncodeError` otherwise).

F-strings implement Python's full format mini-language:
`{value:[[fill]align][sign][#][0][width][grouping][.precision][type]}`.

```dragon
f"{pi:.2f}"    # 3.14        f"{n:08b}"   # 11111111    f"{n:#x}"   # 0xff
f"{x:*^6}"     # **42**      f"{big:,}"   # 1,234,567   f"{r:.1%}"  # 25.0%
```

Quote rule: inside a double-quoted f-string use single-quoted dict keys
(`f"{ages['Ada']}"`) or dot access (`f"{ages.Ada}"`).

`bytes`: literals `b"..."`/`b"\xde\xad"`; `len` is byte count; `b[i]` is an int;
slices are bytes; `+` concatenates; `.hex()` and `bytes.fromhex(s)` round-trip.
Keep text as `str`; drop to `bytes` only at the I/O edge.

## 11. Collections

### list[T]

`append extend insert remove pop index count sort reverse clear copy`; slicing,
`+` concatenation, `[v] * n` prefill, `in` by value, nesting with full type
checking. `sort`/`reverse` in place; `sorted(xs)`/`reversed(xs)` return new
lists; `pop()` removes and returns the last element.

### dict[K, V]

Insertion-ordered. `d[k]` and dot-access `d.key` (sugar for string keys that are
identifiers) both raise `KeyError` when absent; `get(k)` returns `V | None`
(narrow it, or use `or` for a default); `get(k, default)` returns plain `V`.
`setdefault(k, default)` requires the default (the one-arg Python form would
smuggle in None; if you need absence, type the value `V | None`); for heap values
the inserted default IS the returned object, so
`groups.setdefault(k, []).append(x)` buckets correctly. Also: `pop popitem update
clear keys values items`, `del d[k]`, `in` tests keys, iteration yields keys.
`keys()`/`values()` return lists.

### set[T], tuple, deque

Set literals `{1, 2}`; `{}` is an empty DICT, the empty set is `set()`; `set(xs)`
dedupes a list. `add remove discard pop union intersection difference
symmetric_difference issubset issuperset` plus the operator forms (section 8).

Tuples are fixed-size, heterogeneous, immutable: `t: tuple[int, str] = (1, "a")`;
index or unpack (`x, y = point`). The natural multi-value return type.

`from collections import deque`: `appendleft append popleft pop`, O(1) both ends.
(Stdlib, listed here because it completes the container story.)

### Comprehensions

```dragon
squares: list[int]      = [n * n for n in range(10) if n % 2 == 0]
flat:    list[int]      = [x for row in rows for x in row]
lengths: dict[str, int] = {w: len(w) for w in words}
uniq:    set[int]       = {n % 4 for n in nums}
```

A comprehension is an expression; it fills a monomorphized container directly
(no boxing) and goes anywhere a value goes.

## 12. Functions

```dragon
def greet(name: str) -> str {
    return f"Hello, {name}!"
}
```

- Every parameter and the return type are annotated. A function with no result
  is `-> None`; a bare `return` (exit early) is legal only in `-> None`
  functions.
- Multiple values: return a `tuple[...]` and unpack at the call site.
- Defaults: `def connect(host: str = "localhost", port: int = 5432)`. A default
  may be any expression of the parameter type and is re-evaluated ON EACH CALL
  that omits it, so Python's shared-mutable-default trap does not exist.
- Keyword calls: any parameter may be passed by name, reordered, or skipped.
  Unknown keyword names and duplicate positional+keyword bindings are compile
  errors.
- Variadics REQUIRE the element type: `def f(*nums: int)` (a `list[int]` in the
  body), `def g(**opts: str)` (a `dict[str, str]` in the body). An unannotated
  `*args` is a compile error. Order: fixed params, `*args`, `**kwargs`.
- Call-site spread: `f(*list_or_tuple)`, `f(**dict)`, mixes allowed
  (`f(1, *[2, 3])`); a spread feeds a variadic (`inner(*nums)` relays).
- Functions are first-class values typed `Callable[[ArgTs], R]`
  (`Callable[[], None]` for no-arg). Store them in fields, pass them, return
  them, put them in `dict[str, Callable[...]]` dispatch tables.

### Lambdas and closures

```dragon
double: Callable[[int], int] = lambda (n: int) -> int { return n * 2 }
```

Lambda syntax is a typed parameter list, `->` return type, and a BRACED BODY
(full statements allowed, not just one expression). The enclosing binding must
carry the `Callable` type. A non-capturing lambda compiles to a bare function
pointer; a capturing one costs one small environment allocation.

Closures capture enclosing locals by reference. Reading needs nothing; REBINDING
needs `nonlocal` (compile error otherwise). Closures escape correctly: return
them, store them, call them inline (`make_adder(5)(10)`).

## 13. Classes

```dragon
class Counter {
    total: int = 0                  # per-instance field with default
    def(start: int) {               # THE CONSTRUCTOR: a nameless def
        self.total = start
    }
    def bump() -> None {            # method: self is implicit, not declared
        self.total = self.total + 1
    }
}
c: Counter = Counter(10)
```

- **The constructor is `def()`, not `__init__`** in `.dr` (mode-enforced both
  ways; `.py` files use `def __init__(self, ...)`).
- **`self` is implicit in `.dr` methods**: never in the parameter list, always
  available in the body.
- **The field set is fixed at compile time.** Fields are declared (typed,
  optional default) in the class body. Accessing an undeclared member is a
  compile error, not a runtime AttributeError. No runtime attribute creation.
- Non-static field defaults are evaluated FRESH PER INSTANCE (`items: list[str]
  = []` gives every instance its own list; Python's shared class-body mutable
  default trap does not exist).
- Every field without a default must be definitely assigned in the constructor
  (compile-checked, flow-aware).
- A class with no constructor is constructed with NO arguments (fields keep
  defaults); passing arguments is a compile error. Exceptions to this rule are
  the synthesized families: `@dataclass` (field constructor), `TypedDict` (named
  fields), `NamedTuple` (positional fields), exception subclasses (a message),
  and `Enum` (member lookup by value).
- Classes are compile-time entities, not values: you cannot bind a class and
  construct through the binding. But `type` exists as an annotation for
  class-decorator plumbing (`def reg(cls: type) -> type`, `registry: list[type]`)
  and as the parameter type of `assertRaises(exc_type: type, ...)`.

### Class-level state and statics

```dragon
class Circle {
    static total: int = 0             # one value, shared, reached as Circle.total
    static const PI: float = 3.14159  # class constant
    r: float = 0.0

    def(r: float) { self.r = r }

    static def unit() -> Circle {     # factory / alternate constructor
        return Circle(1.0)
    }
    @staticmethod
    def area_of(r: float) -> float {  # same thing, Python spelling
        return Circle.PI * r * r
    }
}
```

`static def` and `@staticmethod` are equivalent (no `self`, called on the
class). `@classmethod` exists and is called on the class (`Widget.create()`);
Dragon resolves classes statically so it is effectively a static factory
(a `@classmethod` GENERATOR is rejected; `async` on `@classmethod` is refused).

### Properties

```dragon
class Temp {
    _c: float = 0.0
    @property
    def celsius() -> float { return self._c }
    @celsius.setter
    def celsius(v: float) -> None { self._c = v }
}
t.celsius = 25.0      # setter; reading t.celsius calls the getter, no parens
```

### Inheritance and dispatch

```dragon
class Dog(Animal) {                  # single inheritance
    def(name: str, breed: str) {
        super(name)                  # delegate to Animal's constructor (opt-in!)
        self.breed = breed
    }
    def speak() -> str {             # override
        return self.name + " barks"
    }
}
```

- `super(args)` calls the parent constructor; `super.method()` calls a parent
  method (`.py`: `super().__init__(...)`, `super().method()`).
- Constructor chaining is OPT-IN: if a subclass constructor never calls
  `super(...)`, the parent constructor does not run. A subclass with no
  constructor is constructed with no arguments (it does not inherit the parent
  signature).
- Virtual dispatch is Python-semantics at C++ cost: a base-typed variable calls
  the most-derived override through one vtable pointer; calls the compiler can
  prove monomorphic are direct. `list[Shape]` of mixed subclasses dispatches per
  element. Prefer overriding over `isinstance` chains.
- `isinstance(obj, Base)` walks the ancestor chain.

### Dunder methods (one parameter shorter than Python: self is implicit)

| To support | Define |
|---|---|
| `print(x)` / `str(x)` / f-strings | `__str__() -> str` (default prints `<Class instance>`) |
| `repr(x)` | `__repr__() -> str` |
| `==` / `!=` | `__eq__(other: T) -> bool` (`!=` derives) |
| `< <= > >=` | `__lt__`, `__le__`, `__gt__`, `__ge__` |
| `+ - *` etc. | `__add__(o: T) -> R`, `__sub__`, `__mul__`, ... |
| `len(x)` | `__len__() -> int` |
| `x[i]` / `x[i] = v` | `__getitem__(i) -> T` / `__setitem__(i, v)` |
| `v in x` | `__contains__(v) -> bool` |
| `for` loops | `__iter__()` + `__next__()` (raise `StopIteration()`) |
| `with` | `__enter__() -> T` + `__exit__() -> None` (see section 19) |
| conversions | `__int__`, `__float__` |

### Member privacy (compile-time enforced, zero runtime cost)

| Prefix | Class member | Module-level name |
|---|---|---|
| `name` | public | public |
| `_name` | protected: declaring class, subclasses, same package | package-internal |
| `__name` | private: the declaring class ONLY (not subclasses) | file-private |
| `__name__` | reserved protocol namespace, public | reserved metadata, public |

Violations are compile errors naming the tier. Defining an unrecognized `__x__`
is a compile error (you cannot fake a reserved name).

### Synthesized class families

```dragon
@dataclass
class Config {
    name: str
    timeout: int = 30
}
c: Config = Config("server")        # field-order constructor, defaults fill
# ==/!= compare by fields; repr(c) formats fields; a user def() overrides synthesis

class Vec(NamedTuple) {
    x: int
    y: int
}
v: Vec = Vec(1, 2)                  # positional construction; v.x, repr work

from enum import Enum, IntEnum, StrEnum, auto
class Color(Enum) {
    RED:   int = 1
    GREEN: int = auto()             # auto() numbers from the previous value
}
Color.RED.name                      # "RED"
Color.GREEN.value                   # 2
Color(2)                            # member lookup by value; ValueError if absent
for c in Color { ... }              # iterates members in definition order
str(Color.RED)                      # "Color.RED"
# Enum compares by identity; IntEnum members compare/order with ints
# (Priority.LOW == 1); StrEnum members compare with strs (FileMode.READ == "r").

enum Token { LPAREN, RPAREN, COMMA }   # native enum: fast int constants
Token.RPAREN                            # 1 (0-based)
```

### Custom exceptions

```dragon
class AppError(Exception) {
    def(message: str) { self.message = message }
}
class NotFoundError(AppError) {
    def(message: str) { self.message = message }
}
```

Subclass `Exception` (or any tree type); catching a base catches the subtypes.
Store structured data in fields and read `e.message` / `e.status` in handlers;
`str(e)` always yields printable text.

## 14. Generics (monomorphized, PEP 695 syntax)

```dragon
def first[T](items: list[T]) -> T { return items[0] }

class Box[T] {
    item: T
    def(x: T) { self.item = x }
    def get() -> T { return self.item }
    def labeled[U](tag: U) -> str { return f"{tag}={self.item}" }   # generic method
}

class Pair[A, B] { ... }                      # multiple parameters

def describe[T: Animal](x: T) -> str {        # class bound: methods of the bound usable,
    return x.speak()                          # dispatches to the concrete override
}
def show[T: Amazing](x: T) -> str { ... }     # contract bound: checked structurally at stamp
def both[T: {Amazing, Speaker}](x: T) { ... } # braced set: must satisfy all
```

- Type arguments infer from the call (`first([10])` solves `T = int`); pin
  explicitly when ambiguous: `first[int](xs)`.
- Each distinct instantiation is stamped as separate native code (visible as
  `first[int]`, `first[str]` symbols). No boxing, no runtime dispatch, no
  erasure.
- **Cross-module generics work**: define a generic function or class in one
  module, import and instantiate it in another.
- Current limits (clean compile errors, never miscompiles):
  - Subclassing a generic instantiation (`class L(Box[str])`) is not supported
    yet.
  - A generic function whose parameter is itself a generic instantiation
    (`def swap[A, B](p: Pair[A, B])`) cannot infer its arguments from the call;
    pin them explicitly (`swap[int, str](p)`).

## 15. Contracts (structural shape, written down)

```dragon
type Amazing {
    def amazing_method() -> str
    def rating() -> int
}
type Performer(Amazing, Speaker) {}      # composition unions the signature sets
```

A contract is a block of bodiless method signatures: no fields, no constructors,
no bodies, no parameter defaults, no empty contracts (each is a compile error).
Conformance is always DECLARED, never inferred from a matching method set:

```dragon
class Dog(Animal) -> Amazing, Speaker { ... }        # producer promise, checked on the class
a: Amazing = robot as Amazing                        # consumer cast, checked at the use site
m: {Amazing, Speaker} = robot as {Amazing, Speaker}  # braced set where a comma would mislead
def duo(x: {Amazing, Speaker}) -> str { ... }        # multi-contract parameter
pack: list[Amazing] = [dog, robot as Amazing]
```

`as` is compile-time only and free (same pointer in and out). A call through a
contract value is one vtable load (the conforming set is closed at whole-program
compile, so there is no Go-style runtime interface conversion cost). A class
with the right methods but no promise/cast is refused with an error offering
both remedies.

Current limits:
- Contracts move upward only: there is no narrowing from a contract-typed
  value back down to its concrete class.
- Keyword arguments on a contract-typed call are refused ("pass positionally").
- Parameterized contracts (`type Iterable[T]`) do not exist; contract BOUNDS on
  generics (`[T: Amazing]`) are the workhorse and monomorphize to direct calls.

## 16. Decorators

`@dec` above a `def` means `f = dec(f)`, run once at definition time. All of the
following are verified working:

```dragon
# Identity/registration decorator
commands: list[Callable[[], None]] = []
def command(f: Callable[[], None]) -> Callable[[], None] {
    commands.append(f)
    return f
}
@command
def deploy() -> None { print("deploying") }

# WRAPPING decorator (returns a new closure around the original)
def timed(f: Callable[[], None]) -> Callable[[], None] {
    return lambda () -> None {
        print("before")
        f()
        print("after")
    }
}

# Decorator FACTORY (parameterized decorator)
def route(path: str) -> Callable[[Callable[[], None]], Callable[[], None]] {
    return lambda (f: Callable[[], None]) -> Callable[[], None] {
        print(f"registered {path}")
        return f
    }
}
@route("/home")
def home() -> None { ... }

# Class decorator (takes and returns `type`); stacks with @dataclass
def reg(cls: type) -> type {
    registry.append(cls)
    return cls
}
@reg
@dataclass
class Item { name: str }
```

A decorator's parameter/return may be spelled as the precise `Callable[...]` or
as bare `ptr` when the shape does not matter. Method decorators: `@property`,
`@name.setter`, `@staticmethod`, `@classmethod` (section 13).

## 17. Iterators and generators

A function containing `yield` is a generator; calling it returns a lazy object
consumed by `for`:

```dragon
def count_up(limit: int) {          # note: no return annotation on generators
    i: int = 0
    while i < limit {
        yield i
        i = i + 1
    }
}
for v in count_up(4) { print(v) }   # 0 1 2 3
```

- State persists across yields; `yield` accepts any expression; generators work
  as methods (instance and `@staticmethod`), not as `@classmethod`.
- There is NO `next()` builtin; the supported consumption is `for`. A generator
  object can only be bound to an `Any`-typed variable; idiomatic use iterates
  the call directly.
- Implement `__iter__() -> Self` and `__next__() -> T` (raising
  `StopIteration()` when done) to make any class for-loopable.

## 18. Exceptions

```dragon
try {
    n: int = int(s)
} except ValueError as e {          # `catch` is an accepted synonym for `except`
    print("bad: " + str(e))
} except (TypeError, KeyError) as e {   # tuple groups types
    ...
} except Exception as e {           # broad net last; matching is hierarchy-based
    ...
} else {
    print("parsed")                 # runs only when the try did not raise
} finally {
    print("done")                   # always runs
}
```

- `raise ValueError("message")` throws; a bare `raise` inside a handler
  re-throws. `raise X from Y` is REFUSED at compile time (no traceback exists to
  chain; the error says to raise without `from`).
- Built-in hierarchy (abridged): `BaseException > Exception >` `ArithmeticError`
  (`ZeroDivisionError`, `OverflowError`, `FloatingPointError`), `LookupError`
  (`IndexError`, `KeyError`), `OSError` (`FileNotFoundError`, `PermissionError`,
  `ConnectionError`...), `RuntimeError` (`NotImplementedError`,
  `RecursionError`), `TypeError`, `ValueError` (`UnicodeError`), `MemoryError`,
  `StopIteration`, `AssertionError`.
- Runtime checks raise catchably: `10 // 0` -> ZeroDivisionError, bad index ->
  IndexError, `d[missing]` -> KeyError, failed allocation -> MemoryError (so a
  server can catch OOM per request and keep serving).
- `assert cond, "msg"` raises a catchable `AssertionError`; uncaught it exits 1
  printing `Unhandled exception: msg`.
- There is no traceback capture (no stack-frame list, no `traceback` module
  render); exceptions are caught and inspected via `except E as e`.
- Exceptions lower to setjmp/longjmp: the throwing path pays, the normal path is
  free. Guarding (`d.get(k, dflt)`, `if b != 0`) still beats catching when
  absence is a normal outcome.

## 19. Context managers (`with`)

```dragon
class Resource {
    def() {}
    def __enter__() -> Resource { print("acquire")  return self }
    def __exit__() -> None { print("release") }
}
with Resource() as r {
    ...                             # __exit__ runs on normal exit AND on raise
}
```

`__exit__` takes no arguments and returns nothing: it is a guaranteed cleanup
hook, not an exception handler. It cannot see or suppress the in-flight
exception; handle errors with `try`/`except` around the `with`.
Locks are context managers: `with lock { ... }` acquires/releases exception-safely.
A `defer` registered inside a `with` body runs before `__exit__`.

## 20. `defer`: scope-exit calls

`fire f(x)` runs f NOW on another green thread; `defer f(x)` runs f LATER on
this thread, when the current BLOCK exits, on every exit path (fall-through,
`return`, `break`, `continue`, exception unwind).

```dragon
def load_index(path: str) -> Index {
    f: Reader = open(path)
    defer f.close()                 # runs on return, raise, every exit
    if bad { raise CorruptionError("bad magic") }   # close still runs
    return parse_index(f)           # close runs after the return value is built
}
```

- **Block-scoped, not function-scoped** (deliberately not Go): a defer in an
  `if` body runs at that `if`'s closing brace; a defer in a loop body runs at
  the end of EVERY iteration. Every defer site is known at compile time and the
  calls are emitted into each exit edge: no runtime defer stack, no allocation.
- Within one scope, defers run LIFO, and before the scope's own memory cleanup.
- **Arguments are snapshots**: arguments (and a method defer's receiver)
  evaluate AT THE DEFER STATEMENT; only the call runs at exit. `defer log(i)`
  then `i = 9` still logs the old value. To see the latest state, defer a call
  that reads a borrowed container (`defer report(stats)`); mutations to the
  SAME object are visible, rebinding the NAME is not.
- Ownership composes: `defer archive(own log)` moves at the statement (later
  uses are use-after-move errors); `defer send(dub items)` snapshots a copy. A
  plain borrowed argument is PINNED: you may read/mutate it, but `own`-moving or
  `del`-ing it while a defer holds it is a compile error.
- Defers run during unwind, before the handler sees the exception. If NO handler
  exists and the process dies on an uncaught exception, pending defers are
  skipped (defer is a scope tool, not a shutdown hook).
- Current limits (loud compile errors): no `defer` at module top level
  ("'defer' outside function"); the operand must BE a direct function/method
  call (bare names refuse; computed/closure callees refuse); no keyword
  arguments in the deferred call (pass positionally); `Any`/union-typed
  arguments refuse (annotate concretely); no `defer fire f(x)` fusion.

## 21. Ownership: `del`, `own`, `dub`

All three resolve at compile time; every misuse is a line-numbered compile
error, never a runtime behavior change. A program using none of them compiles
exactly as before. One rule underneath: every heap value has exactly one OWNER;
every other name is a borrow that must not outlive it.

Binding states: `Owned` (sole owner) -> `Owned*` (still sole owner but aliased/
escaped; del and move refuse, naming the escape site) -> `Moved` / `Deleted`
(name is dead; any use is an error naming where it died). Fresh values (literals,
constructors, concat results, `dub` copies) are Owned; element/field reads and
plain parameters are Borrowed. A plain call argument stays a borrow (calls do
not consume).

### `del x` (proven early free)

Frees the value ON THAT LINE, compiling only when the compiler proves `x` is the
sole owner (nothing aliased, stored, or captured it; otherwise the error names
the escape). Use it to cut peak memory and as a lifetime probe: every refusal
reveals an alias you did not know about. Debug builds lower it to
`assert(rc == 1); free` (a leak trips loudly); release builds emit exactly the
scope-exit release, earlier. v1 scope: LOCALS inside functions only; `del` or
`own`-moves of module-level bindings refuse ("v1: locals only"). Fields cannot
be `del`-ed (they release when their owner dies). `del d["key"]` (container
entry) is a separate, ordinary operation.

### `own` (sole ownership)

- **On a field**: `own _ctx: TlsCtx` makes the instance the sole owner; the
  generated cleanup releases the field when the instance dies (depth-first
  through owned chains; no destructor methods, no finalizers). MANDATORY for
  raw resource types: a plain `Lock` field is a compile error ("must be declared
  own"), and containers of raw resources refuse (wrap the resource in a class).
  `own` fields form a tree, so they are excluded from cycle-GC tracking.
- **On a collection-typed field** it seals BOTH edges: a borrow cannot be stored
  in, and the field cannot be returned/aliased out (`return self.headers` is a
  compile error; expose methods, or `return dub self.headers`). `str`/`bytes`/
  `tuple`/`ptr` fields are not sealed (immutable payloads are indistinguishable
  from copies; `ptr` is already the on-your-own type).
- **On a parameter AND at the call site** (both ends must say it):
  `def set(own d: dict[str, str])` + `cfg.set(own headers)`. After the call the
  source name is dead. `cfg.set(dub headers)` also satisfies an `own` parameter
  (hands over a fresh copy, keeps yours). An UNMARKED binding at an own
  parameter is an error; `own` at a borrowing parameter is an error; a nameless
  fresh temp (`cfg.set({...})`) needs no keyword. Keyword position works:
  `cfg.set(d=own headers)`.
- **On returns**: inference reads the body; a function whose every return mints
  a fresh value hands the caller sole ownership. `-> own T` pins the promise
  (checked, never inserts a copy); returning a still-held value from a `-> own`
  function is an error. `return own s` is refused (a return already hands over
  its reference). Moving/`del`-ing a call result the callee still owns is an
  error (declare the callee `-> own` or `dub`).

### `dub` (the priced copy)

The only way to mint a second independent owner; the cost sits on the line you
wrote (grep `dub` to find every copy). Deep copy for list/dict/set (recursively,
if every element is dubable); trivial for scalars; a free retain for str/bytes/
tuple (immutable, indistinguishable from a copy). Non-dubable: `Lock`
("a raw OS mutex cannot be copied"), handle-holding classes (composition rule:
a class is dubable iff every field is), Task handles. `dub` copies a binding or
a field, not an element (`dub xs[0]` refuses).

### The three MANDATORY places

1. `own` on a raw-resource field (E15/E16): without a static owner a Lock leaks.
2. `del` to close a conditional move (E9): if one branch moves a value, every
   path must consume it (move, or `del` where there is nothing to move into);
   there are no runtime drop flags. Loops: a binding defined outside a loop
   cannot be consumed inside it (E10); define it inside the loop.
3. `dub` to iterate a container the loop body mutates (E17):
   `for x in dub xs { xs.remove(x) }`. Mutating the iterated container without
   the snapshot is a compile error (silent skipping refused). Works identically
   for fields (`for n in dub self.names`).

### The fire boundary and the task result

Every heap value crossing INTO a green thread goes through one of five doors,
else compile error E12 at the fire line:

```dragon
fire handle(own req)      # 1 moved: the thread is sole owner; req dead here; NON-ATOMIC refcounts by proof
fire tally(dub counts)    # 2 copied: the thread gets its own
fire banner(GREETING)     # 3 immortal const string literal
fire record(router)       # 4 internally-locked type (own Lock field guards its storage)
fire read_only(counts)    # 5 shared read-only: compiles ONLY if the callee provably never writes it
```

A plain `fire f(x)` that compiles is PROOF `f` only reads `x`; a `dub` at a fire
site is a visible admission a copy was needed.

Coming OUT: the result slot is an `own` field of the Task. `await t` / `t.join()`
MOVE the result out exactly once (a second await/join is an error). An abandoned
bound task detaches at scope exit and its unclaimed result is freed with the
task. `del t` (inside a function) is the honest spelling of fire-and-forget for
a held handle. A bare async call in statement position whose Task nobody binds
is a compile error ("silently discarded; bind it, then await, join, or del
it"); `fire fn()` in statement position stays legal (the keyword IS the visible
detach). `dub t` on a task refuses (a second joiner would race the move).

### Ownership error codes

E1 use-after-move, E2 use-after-del, E3 del of a field, E4 del/move of a borrow,
E5 del/move of an escaped value (names the escape site), E7 del/move of a
closure-captured binding, E8 storing a borrow into an own field, E9 conditional
move (consume on every path or none), E10 move inside a loop of an outer
binding, E11 dub of a non-dubable type, E12 unproven value crossing a fire
boundary, E13 plain call to an own parameter, E14 own at a borrowing parameter,
E15 raw-resource field without own, E16 raw resource as container element, E17
mutating a container while iterating it, E18 del/move of a call result the
callee still owns, E19 a `-> own` function returning a still-held value, E20
`own` on a returned expression, E21 returning an own field.

## 22. Concurrency (colorless: no function coloring, ever)

Three tiers share one scheduler and one suspension rule: a pause is visible at
the call site, but never changes a function's signature.

| Tier | Syntax | Backed by | For |
|---|---|---|---|
| Green thread | `fire fn()` / `fire { }` | M:N scheduler (~64KB each; spawn thousands) | tasks, I/O fan-out |
| Scoped OS thread | `thread { }` | one OS thread, auto-joins at scope exit | CPU bursts |
| Manual OS thread | `Thread(target=fn)` | one OS thread you start/join | daemons, custom lifecycles |

### `fire` and `Task[T]`

```dragon
t: Task[int] = fire compute(9)     # the annotation is REQUIRED (a bare = or := binding refuses)
r: int = t.join()                  # wait and take the result (consumes the task)
alive: bool = t.is_alive()
fire log_event("x", 7)             # fire-and-forget a call
fire { cleanup()  notify() }       # fire-and-forget a block
```

`t: Task = fire f()` (bare Task) is accepted; the inner type is pinned for you.
When a green thread does I/O the runtime parks it and runs another. Fan-out is
several fires, then join each.

### `async def` and `await`

`async def f(...) -> T` changes only the RETURN: calling it yields a `Task[T]`
running on a green thread. `await` unwraps a Task and is legal in ANY function
(the caller does not become async; there is no event loop, no `asyncio.run`, one
Lock/one read of everything):

```dragon
async def fetch(url: str) -> str { ... }

def show() -> None {               # NOT async, and that is fine
    data: str = await fetch("https://x.com")   # run and wait
    t: Task[str] = fetch("...")                # no await: hold the handle
    later: str = await t
}
```

`await` on a non-Task is a compile error. `fire` reads as "run this in
parallel"; `async`/`await` reads as "this does I/O, pause me here": same
machinery. Async methods work; `async` on `@classmethod` is refused.

### OS threads

```dragon
thread {                            # contextual keyword; real OS thread
    heavy_work()
}                                   # joined HERE, guaranteed (RAII for threads)

from threading import Thread
t: Thread = Thread(target=worker)
t.start()  t.is_alive()  t.join()   # join returns an int status, NOT a value
```

You can `await` inside a `thread { }` block (a green thread on top of it). Want
a typed result? That is what `fire`/`Task[T]` is for.

### Synchronization (`from threading import ...`)

All acquire the same way: `acquire()` blocks; `acquire(blocking=False)` tries
once and returns bool; `acquire(blocking=True, timeout=T)` waits up to T
seconds. All are context managers (`with lock { ... }` is acquire/release made
exception-safe). Every primitive holds its OS state in an `own` field and
releases when its holder dies; each also has an idempotent `destroy()`.

| Primitive | Construct | Key methods | Use |
|---|---|---|---|
| `Lock` | `Lock()` | acquire/release/with | mutual exclusion |
| `RWLock` | `RWLock()` | `acquire()` shared read; `acquire(write=True)` exclusive; with = write | many readers or one writer |
| `Semaphore` | `Semaphore(n)` | acquire/release | bound concurrency to n |
| `Barrier` | `Barrier(n)` | `wait()` | rendezvous n threads |
| `Condition` | `Condition()` | acquire/wait/notify/notify_all/release | wait for a state change |
| `Event` | `Event()` | set/clear/is_set/wait | broadcast flag |

The idiomatic shape keeps data and its lock together, and the `own` is
compiler-mandated:

```dragon
class Counter {
    value: int = 0
    own lock: Lock = Lock()
    def bump() -> None {
        with self.lock { self.value = self.value + 1 }
    }
}
```

Guarding a module global instead: declare `global counter` in the writer, then
`with lock { counter = counter + 1 }`.

`from collections.concurrent import ConcurrentList, ConcurrentDict`: minimal
internally-locked containers (int elements / str->int; method access:
append/get/set/len/pop). For other shapes, guard a normal container with a Lock.

## 23. Templates and content types (compile-time, zero-cost)

`template { ... }` is raw literal text with two sigils; it compiles to a chain
of string concatenations (literals in .rodata + your values). No engine, no
runtime parsing; a typo in `!{naem}` is a compile error.

> `!{expr}` breaks OUT of content into Dragon code.
> `:{ ... }` breaks BACK into content from inside that code.

```dragon
greeting: str = template {Hello !{name}, you are !{age}}     # untyped -> plain str
```

- **Bare `{ }` are literal text** (not an f-string): JSON/CSS/code emit without
  escaping ceremony. The lexer tracks brace depth; for stray braces `!!}` is a
  literal `}` and `!!{` a literal `!{` (the only escapes; a bare `!!` is plain
  text). Unterminated `!{` or invalid Dragon inside it is a compile error.
- Whitespace/newlines inside the block are preserved verbatim.

### Typed templates auto-escape

`template[HTML] { ... }` escapes every `!{}` through `HTML.escape()` and returns
the type `HTML`, not `str`: XSS-safe by default, and the compiler refuses a SQL
value where an HTML page belongs. Unwrap with `.to_str()` (or `str(x)`).

Ship-in types: `HTML`, `CSS`, `XML` (`from html import ...`), `JSON`
(`from json import JSON`), `SQL` (`from database import SQL`), `URL`
(`from urllib.parse import URL`).

Splice dispatch inside a typed template: same content type -> inserted RAW (no
double-escape; this is why components compose); `str` -> escaped; a class ->
`__str__()` (or `__repr__()`) then escaped; a container -> the same text
`f"{xs}"` gives, then escaped; `Any` -> dispatched at runtime, then escaped;
`!{x | raw}` -> verbatim (explicit opt-out). A class with neither `__str__` nor
`__repr__` has no text form and the splice is a compile error naming the fix
(section 13); the same rule governs `print`, `str()`, and f-strings.

Components and layouts are just functions:

```dragon
def button(label: str) -> HTML {
    return template[HTML] { <button>!{label}</button> }
}
ui: HTML = template[HTML] { <div>!{button("Save")}</div> }
```

Logic: full statements inside `!{ }`, `:{ }` to emit markup, nesting allowed,
content type inherited:

```dragon
lst: HTML = template[HTML] {<ul>!{ for x in xs { :{<li>!{x}</li>} } }</ul>}
```

Inline shortcuts: `!{*xs}` spreads a list (empty separator); `!{xs | join(", ")}`.
Filters: `html`/`sql`/`url` (context escapes), `raw`, `join`/`join(sep)`, or any
in-scope TOP-LEVEL `(str) -> str` function (`!{title | shout}`); methods do not
work as filters (wrap `upper` in a function).

`template[HTML]("page.html")` is a compile-time include: the file is read during
compilation, its `!{}` slots compile against the surrounding scope, zero runtime
I/O. Path must be a string literal, relative to the source file.

Custom content types: subclass `Template`, give it a `@staticmethod escape(s:
str) -> str`, then `template[SHELL] { ... }` works like the built-ins.

### `SQL` and `URL` are structural, not textual

In `template[SQL]`, `!{}` does NOT paste text: it becomes a bound parameter
(`$$0`, `$$1`, ...) with the value carried alongside, so injection is impossible
by construction. Limits: each `!{}` must be a single bound expression (no
`!{ for }` / `!{ if }` blocks in SQL), and splicing one SQL value into another
is a compile error. In `template[URL]`, `!{}` percent-encodes the query-string
characters (space, `&`, `=`, `?`, `#`, `+`); for full RFC 3986 encoding use
`!{v | url}` or `urllib.parse.quote(v, "")`.

Repo rule: never hand-build HTML/JSON/SQL by string concatenation; use the
content-type system (parameterized `db.prepare(sql)` with `?` binds is fine).

## 24. Modules, packages, eggs

`import` is a compile-time directive (no runtime module objects, no
`sys.modules`, no per-import cost):

```dragon
import math                    # math.gcd(12, 18)
from math import gcd           # gcd(12, 18)
import math as m               # alias a module
from math import gcd as g     # alias a name
```

- A sibling file `shapes.dr` next to the entry file imports as `from shapes
  import area`.
- **Package rule**: a package is a directory whose root module is
  `name/name.dr`; submodules are files beside it (`textkit/case.dr` imports as
  `textkit.case`). Dots map to directory separators. No `__init__` files. A
  flat `name.dr` AND a `name/` directory in the same place is a hard error
  ("module conflict"), never a precedence guess.
- Resolution order, first match wins: (1) the entry file's directory, (2) `-I`
  dirs in order, (3) the stdlib (`DRAGON_STDLIB_PATH` overrides), (4) Python
  site-packages if `--site-packages`. Your files shadow the stdlib (closest
  wins), which is also how you stub a module in tests.
- Module globals: a top-level declaration; functions read freely, writers must
  declare `global name`. A dependency's loose statements do not run; its
  initializers do (once, at startup, before the entry body): express import-time
  setup as `const X = setup()`.
- Module-level privacy: `_name` is package-internal, `__name` file-private,
  enforced at import (`from bank import _rate` from outside the package is a
  compile error).
- There is no importlib/reload/`getattr`-on-module. Selecting behavior by a
  runtime string is a typed dispatch table: `ops: dict[str, Callable[[int],
  int]]`, populated at top level; plugins register themselves into it. Code
  never compiled in cannot be loaded, by design.
- **Eggs (D022)**: `dragon init` scaffolds `dragon.drs` (Dragon Script: `#`
  comments, single quotes, nested `{ }` tables; fields: name, version, entry,
  eggs = {}). The full manager (grab/sync/drop with inline SHA-256 pins in the
  manifest, `.drx/` install dir, capability permissions) is designed and partly
  wired but in progress: TODAY YOU VENDOR (copy sources, or `-I`). Lay shared
  code out as `name/name.dr` so it is egg-shaped later. No install scripts
  exist, ever: a task is a `.dr` file you `dragon run`.

## 25. FFI

### In-process lane: `extern "C"`

```dragon
extern "C" from "curl" {                 # binds a library; auto-links -lcurl
    def curl_easy_init() -> ptr
    def curl_easy_perform(h: ptr) -> intc
    def curl_easy_cleanup(h: ptr)
}
extern "C" def sqrt(x: float) -> float   # a symbol the linker already sees
print(sqrt(2.0))                         # called exactly like a Dragon function
```

Type map (a bound call is a DIRECT native call; nothing to marshal for the
trivial core):

| Dragon | C | Note |
|---|---|---|
| `int` | `int64_t` | if the C header says `long long`/`int64_t`, use `int` |
| `intc` | `int` (32-bit) | if the C header says `int`/`unsigned`, use `intc`; auto-widens back in Dragon |
| `float` | `double` | |
| `bool` | `bool` | |
| `ptr` | `void*` | opaque handles; GC never touches it; `none` passes NULL |
| `str` | `const char*` | valid as a C string FOR ASCII ONLY (see below) |
| `bytes` | `(uint8_t*, int64_t)` | the vehicle for binary and non-ASCII text |

Strings: ASCII `str` passes straight through zero-copy. Non-ASCII `str` is
stored UCS-4 internally: convert first via the runtime ABI, and mind the
LIFETIME RULE: a pointer from `dragon_bytes_data(buf)` is valid only while `buf`
is alive, so bind the bytes to a variable that outlives the call (never inline
the conversion and stash the pointer).

Runtime ABI (statically linked into every binary; a stability contract):

```dragon
extern "C" def dragon_string_alloc(src: ptr, length: int) -> str   # C string -> Dragon str (copies)
extern "C" def dragon_str_to_utf8_bytes(s: str) -> bytes           # NUL-terminated UTF-8
extern "C" def dragon_str_encode(s: str) -> bytes                  # str.encode(): UTF-8
extern "C" def dragon_bytes_new(data: ptr, len: int) -> bytes
extern "C" def dragon_bytes_data(b: bytes) -> ptr
extern "C" def dragon_bytes_len(b: bytes) -> int
extern "C" def dragon_raise_exc(code: int, msg: str)               # raise a Dragon exception from C
```

Out-parameters: Dragon has NO address-of operator, on purpose. Two patterns:
(1) opaque C-filled state: `malloc` scratch, pass the `ptr`, never look inside,
free at the end (how stdlib/threading.dr drives pthread mutexes); (2) scalar
out-params: flatten in a one-line C shim that returns the value, compiled via
`--cc-source shim.c`.

C++: no direct binding (name mangling is not a stable ABI). Write a small
`extern "C"` shim (.cpp) exposing plain C entry points over opaque pointers;
pass it with `--cc-source cvshim.cpp` plus `-I`/`-l` flags; the presence of any
`.cpp` automatically switches the final link driver from `cc` to `c++`.

Ownership discipline at the boundary: scalars/`ptr` carry no ownership; a
str/bytes passed into C is borrowed for that one call (C copies if it keeps
it); values from the Dragon constructors above are Dragon-owned and scope-freed;
a C library's own allocations are C-owned: hold the `ptr`, call the library's
destructor yourself.

Link troubleshooting: `undefined reference` -> missing `-l` or missing
`--cc-source` shim; `std::...` undefined -> C++ shim not passed via --cc-source;
header not found -> `-I`; `cannot find -l` -> `-L` or install the dev package;
garbled text in C -> you passed non-ASCII `str` directly; crash after a call ->
the backing str/bytes went out of scope.

### Process lane: `extern "python" | "golang" | "rust"`

```dragon
extern "python" def score(batch: list[User]) -> list[Scored] from "ml/score.py"
```

Typed at compile time; `Any` appears nowhere unless you ask for it. Lowering:
arguments encode as one JSON object via the box-free `encode[T]` (a `bytes`
parameter rides as a raw blob, never base64); the child spawns ONCE from an
argv list (never a shell) and stays warm (imports and state survive across
calls); one length-prefixed frame per call on stdin/stdout; the reply decodes
via `decode[T]` into the declared return type. The pipe wait parks the green
thread, so plain calls cooperate and `fire score(users)` fans out; there is no
async/await ceremony. A child that answers with an error keeps serving (you get
its traceback); a dead child restarts for the NEXT call only (a crash never
silently re-runs side effects). Python resolution: `DRAGON_PYTHON` env var or
absolute candidates (`/usr/bin/python3`, ...), never a `$PATH` search. For
golang/rust the `from` path IS the compiled binary, relative to the declaring
`.dr` file.

`dragon ffi sync file.dr` reads the extern declarations (the Dragon signature
is the IDL) and generates per-language counterparts: `*_stub.py` (dataclasses +
serve loop, always regenerated, do not edit) plus a skeleton written once that
is yours. `dragon check` and every build fail when a stub's embedded signature
no longer matches the declaration; `dragon ffi sync file.dr --check` verifies
byte-exactly in CI. One script/binary per extern (a stub owned by another `.dr`
file is never overwritten).

Errors (`from ffi import FFIError, SpawnError, ForeignError, SchemaError`):
SpawnError (could not start), ForeignError (child raised; carries the child's
stderr/traceback verbatim), SchemaError (clean exit, output did not match the
declared type). `except FFIError` catches all three. No decode is attempted
after a non-zero exit.

The kernel under the sugar is public: `runs[T](argv, input_bytes)` spawns a
one-shot process, feeds stdin, decodes stdout into `T` with the same error
taxonomy. `encode[T]`/`decode[T]` are the same public schema-directed
serializer pair, usable directly on any class.

Current limits: crossing types are scalars, `list` of scalars, classes, `list`
of classes, `dict[str, scalar]`, `Optional` of those, and `bytes` as a
parameter/return (not yet as a class field). `dict` with non-str keys or class
values: not yet. Calls on one sidecar serialize in call order (no worker pools
yet). A reply of unknown shape: declare `-> dict[str, Any]` and narrow (boxed
tier, priced by the `Any` in the signature).

Price model: serialize + pipe + deserialize per call; spawn and imports paid
once. Right for batch-shaped calls; wrong for per-element calls in a hot loop
(use the C lane against the underlying native library instead).

## 26. Built-in functions (always in scope, no import)

Available: `print(*values)` (space-separated, newline), `input(prompt="")`,
`len`, `range(stop | start,stop[,step])` (iteration construct, not an
annotation type), `enumerate`, `zip`, `int` `float` `str` `bool` (conversions),
`bytes()` / `bytes(n)` / `bytes(list[int])`, `abs` `min` `max` `sum`, `sorted`
`reversed` (return new lists), `any` `all`, `isinstance` (see the caveat in
section 7), `issubclass(Sub, Base)`, `repr` `id` `hash`, `chr` `ord`, `hex`
`oct` `bin`, `set()` / `set(xs)`, `dict()`, `list()` (empty -> `list[Any]`
only) and `list(iterable)` (typed copy: `xs: list[int] = list([1, 2, 3])`
works), `tuple(iterable)`, `pow(int, int)`, `round(x: float) -> int` (1-arg
ONLY), `divmod(a, b) -> tuple[int, int]`, `map(fn, xs)` / `filter(fn, xs)`
(EAGER, return lists; comprehensions are the idiomatic form), `type(x) -> str`
(the type NAME, not a class object), `dir(obj) -> list[str]` (method names,
including `__init__`; how the unittest runner finds `test_*` methods).

Not part of the surface: 2-arg `round(x, n)`, `bytes("text", "utf-8")`
(use `s.encode()`), `next`, `iter`, `callable`, `frozenset`, `format`, `slice`,
`getattr`, `setattr`, `hasattr`, `delattr`, `vars`, `globals`, `locals`,
`eval`, `exec`, `compile` (the reflection/eval family is rejected by design,
not a gap).

An empty `list()` is a `list[Any]` and so cannot initialize a concretely typed
binding; the typed empty list is the literal: `xs: list[int] = []`.

## 27. Testing

A test file is an ordinary Dragon program run with `dragon run`; the framework
is `stdlib/unittest.dr` (pure Dragon). No discovery harness, no magic naming,
no test subcommand: the file's last line runs the suite, and the process exit
code (0/1) is the whole contract for CI/CTest.

```dragon
from unittest import TestCase, main

class MathTests(TestCase) {
    def test_addition() {
        self.assertEqual(2 + 2, 4)
    }
    def test_overdraw_raises() {
        self.assertRaises(ValueError, overdraw)     # callable form only
    }
}

main([MathTests()])          # INSTANCES, not classes; returns the exit status
```

- Tests are `test_*` methods (no parameters); they run in ALPHABETICAL order
  (via `dir()`), so tests must not depend on sequence.
- A missed assertion is a FAILURE; an uncaught exception in a test body is an
  ERROR (reported, counted, the run continues). Summary: `Ran N test(s);
  failures: F, errors: E` then `OK`/`FAILED`, exit 0/1.
- Assertion catalog (each takes a trailing `msg: str = ""`):
  `assertEqual[T](a, b)` and `assertNotEqual[T](a, b)`: GENERIC, so comparing
  mismatched concrete types (`assertEqual(1, "x")`) is a COMPILE error, and
  equality is deep for containers; `assertTrue/assertFalse(bool)`;
  `assertIsNone/assertIsNotNone(Any)`; `assertIn/assertNotIn(needle, haystack)`
  (substring on str); `assertGreater/Less/GreaterEqual/LessEqual(float)`;
  `assertAlmostEqual(a, b, places=7)`; `assertRaises(exc_type, fn)` (pass a
  zero-arg function or lambda; subclass matches count; there is NO
  context-manager form).
- No auto `setUp`/`tearDown`: write a helper method and call it at the top of
  each test (explicit per-test isolation).
- Project convention (this repo, and recommended): value-assertable runtime
  behavior goes in dogfooded `.dr` unittests under `test/dr/test_*.dr` (CMake
  auto-globs them into ctest as `dr_<name>`; run `ctest -R dr_`). C++
  GoogleTest is reserved for compiler internals: stdout-capture assertions and
  must-not-compile checks (`checkHasErrors`). Gtest source programs live in
  `test/cases/<TestFileName>.md` case files (a `#### :snake_case` heading, then
  a `dr`/`py`-tagged fence), pulled by `extractCode`: never hand-built from
  glued C++ string literals.

## 28. Memory model and performance

- Scalars (`int`, `float`, `bool`) are register values; heap types (str, bytes,
  containers, instances, closures) are references: assignment copies the
  handle, both names see the same object.
- Automatic reference counting with DETERMINISTIC frees: the count drops at
  scope exit, reassignment, or last use, and the object frees immediately, on
  that line. Every block is a scope, so a heap value declared in a block dies
  at the closing brace. No pauses, no collector thread on the hot path.
- Exceptions unwind and free owned locals as they go; error paths do not leak.
- Cycles (A <-> B) are reclaimed by an incremental cycle collector backstop
  (with adaptive back-off when it finds nothing); `own` fields form trees and
  are excluded from cycle tracking.
- `--gc=none` disables refcount emission for one-shot programs (benchmarks,
  short CLI filters); never ship a service with it.
- Performance levers: build `--release` for anything measured; keep types
  concrete (a value left `Any` is boxed; a knowable type in a register is not);
  never build strings with `out = out + x` in a loop (O(n^2)): collect
  `list[str]` and `"".join(parts)`; `del` big buffers early to cut peak memory;
  `fire f(own x)` keeps refcounts non-atomic by proof.
- Measured reality: compute-bound code (recursion, float loops) runs at parity
  with Rust at `-O2`; current known gaps are allocation-churn and str-keyed
  dict workloads (runtime data structures, being driven down).

## 29. What Dragon rejects (deltas from Python, one table)

| Python habit | Dragon answer |
|---|---|
| gradual/optional typing | types are mandatory, both surfaces |
| `x = 1` creates a variable | `x: int = 1` declares; bare `=` only reassigns |
| function-scoped `if`/`for` locals | block scoping; hoist a declaration before the block |
| forgot-`nonlocal` silently makes a local | compile error naming both fixes |
| `if __name__ == "__main__"` | delete it; the file you run is the program |
| arbitrary-precision int | 64-bit `int`, wraps silently |
| truthy `if x:` on any value | conditions are `bool`; write `if x != 0`, `if len(s) > 0` |
| mutable default evaluated once | defaults re-evaluate per call; trap gone |
| `eval` / `exec` / `compile` | absent, by design |
| `getattr`/`setattr`/`hasattr`/`__dict__`/metaclass/monkeypatch | absent; declare fields, or keep dynamism in a `dict[str, V]` value |
| `type(x)` returns a class to compare/construct | `type(x)` is a name string; narrow with `isinstance`, dispatch via base class or generics |
| duck typing | declare it: a base class, or a contract (`type Shape { ... }` + promise/cast) |
| `importlib` / plugins from disk | typed `dict[str, Callable]` dispatch tables; the compiled-in set is the universe |
| `async def` colors callers | colorless: `await` anywhere, `fire` spawns, no event loop |
| pip / PyPI / C extensions | statically linked stdlib; FFI to C; vendor sources (eggs in progress) |
| `raise X from Y` | refused; fold the cause into the new message |
| tracebacks | none; exceptions carry your fields and `str(e)` |
| `del x` unbinds a name | `del x` PROVES sole ownership and frees on that line |
| implicit copies/aliases | copies are spelled `dub`, moves are spelled `own`, both greppable |

Porting checklist for a `.py` file: annotate everything; first bindings get
`:`; hoist names read after a block; replace dynamic features; drop the
`__main__` guard; swap asyncio for `fire`/`await`; verify the imports exist in
the Dragon stdlib. A file that leans on something with no equivalent is not
portable, and that is the honest answer.
